WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.730
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:08.790
Have you heard the saying that whenever we
look into the mirror, strictly speaking, we

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:16.170
don’t really see ourselves, but we see ourselves
from the past…from a few nanoseconds ago.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.170
Is that true?

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:19.060
If so, why?

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.821
This is indeed true, and the reason for this
is that the speed of light is finite, and

00:00:23.821 --> 00:00:27.140
it has to travel back from the mirror to our
eyes.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:32.020
If you feel that this is really hard to imagine,
you are in luck, because a legendary paper

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:36.760
from 2013 by the name Femto-photography captured
this effect.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:42.469
I would say it is safe to start holding on
to your papers from this point basically until

00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:43.950
the end of this video.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:49.660
Here you can see a super high-speed camera
capturing how a wave of light propagates through

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:55.539
a bottle, most makes it through, and some
gets absorbed by the bottle cap.

00:00:55.539 --> 00:01:00.560
But this means that this mirror example we
talked about shall not only be a thought experiment,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.390
but we can even witness it ourselves.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:07.100
Yup, toy first, mirror image second.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.990
Approximately a nanosecond apart.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:14.700
So if someone says that you look old, you
have an excellent excuse now.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.530
The first author of this work was Andreas
Velten, who worked on this at MIT, and he

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:25.380
is now a professor leading an incredible research
group at the University of Wisconsin, Madison.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:30.710
But wait…since it is possible to create
light transport simulations, in which we simulate

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:36.159
the path of many-many millions of light rays
to create a beautiful, photorealistic image,

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:41.189
Adrián Jarabo thought that he would create
a simulator that wouldn’t just give us the

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:48.430
final image, but he would show us the propagation
of light in a digital, simulated environment.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:53.079
As you see here, with this, we can create
even crazier experiments because we are not

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:59.109
limited to the real-world light conditions
and the limitations of the camera.

00:01:59.109 --> 00:02:02.619
The beauty of this technique is just unparalleled.

00:02:02.619 --> 00:02:07.759
He calls this method transient rendering,
and this particular work is tailored to excel

00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:09.700
at rendering caustic patterns.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:16.500
A caustic is a beautiful phenomenon in nature
where curved surfaces reflect or refract light,

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.080
thereby concentrating it to a relatively small
area.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.830
I hope that you are not surprised when I say
that it is the favorite phenomenon of most

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.099
light transport researchers.

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:29.349
Now, a word about these caustics.

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:32.840
We need a super efficient technique to be
able to pull this off.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:39.150
For instance, back in 2013, we showcased a
fantastic scene made by Vlad Miller that was

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:44.659
a nightmare to compute, and it took a community
effort and more than a month to accomplish

00:02:44.659 --> 00:02:45.659
it.

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:50.989
Beyond that, the transient renderer only uses
very little memory, builds on the photon beams

00:02:50.989 --> 00:02:56.700
technique we talked about a few videos ago,
and always arrives to a correct solution,

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.390
given enough time.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:02:59.660
Bravo!

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.010
And we can do all this, through the power
of science.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:05.670
Isn’t it incredible?

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:09.950
And if you feel a little stranded at home
and are yearning to learn more about light

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:14.989
transport, I held a Master-level course on
light transport simulations at the Technical

00:03:14.989 --> 00:03:16.860
University of Vienna.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:21.660
Since I was always teaching it to a handful
of motivated students, I thought that the

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.470
teachings shouldn’t only be available for
the privileged few who can afford a college

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:30.620
education, but the teachings should be available
for everyone.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:36.299
So, the course is now available free of charge
for everyone, no strings attached, so make

00:03:36.299 --> 00:03:39.610
sure to click the link in the video description
to get started.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:44.501
We write a full light simulation program from
scratch there, and learn about physics, the

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:47.010
world around us, and more.

